INT. ORBITAL SALVAGE TUG - COCKPIT - SHIP TIME 03:12

Cramped. Warm. Handwritten sticky notes on every panel. Through the viewport: the husk of a dead freighter, tumbling slowly.

PILOT KESTREL VANDERHOOF (30s, shaved head, a tattoo of a moth on her neck) flicks switches. At the engineering station, DR. OSEI MENSAH-RIVERA (40s, glasses taped at the hinge, calm voice) frowns at a readout. And the ship's AI, a soft synthetic voice named PEBBLE, speaks from the ceiling.

KESTREL
Pebble, scan that hull again.

PEBBLE (V.O.)
Scanning. The hull contains residual heat signatures.

OSEI
Residual from what?

PEBBLE (V.O.)
Possibly from life.

KESTREL
There shouldn't be life.

OSEI
There shouldn't be anything.

PEBBLE (V.O.)
Respectfully, there is both.

Kestrel leans toward the glass. Osei rises and joins her. Together they watch the freighter turn. A single window glows amber.

OSEI
I count one.

KESTREL
I count more.

PEBBLE (V.O.)
I count seventeen.

OSEI
Seventeen what?

PEBBLE (V.O.)
Heartbeats.

Kestrel and Osei stare at the speaker. At each other. At the glass.

KESTREL
Seventeen heartbeats on a ship that's been dead six years.

OSEI
Could be a malfunction.

KESTREL
Could be a rescue.

OSEI
Could be a trap.

KESTREL
Could be both.

She unbuckles. Pushes off toward the airlock. He grabs her ankle. She grabs his wrist. They float, face to face.

KESTREL (CONT'D)
^Let go.

OSEI
^Don't.

PEBBLE (V.O.)
Please do not argue in zero-g. It increases the risk of injury.

Kestrel laughs despite herself. Osei lets go.

OSEI
We call it in first.

KESTREL
Fine.

She floats back to the console. Keys the comm. Static. A man's voice answers from the freighter, calm, cheerful, impossible.

VOICE (V.O.)
Salvage tug, welcome. We've been waiting.

Kestrel looks at Osei. Osei looks at the ceiling.

OSEI
Pebble?

PEBBLE (V.O.)
I do not recognize that voice.

Kestrel's hand hovers over the airlock release.
